# Cognitive Persistence Agent

**Author:** Vyshak


**Version 9.5.3**

Cognitive Persistence Agent (CPA) is a local, project-scoped agent runtime that combines persistent canonical cognition, semantic/structural retrieval, tool-using chat, an isolated workspace, review-gated source changes, Git-backed project history, document/code ingestion, reusable agents/workflows, and multiple LLM providers.

Version 9.5.3 is the current v9.x snapshot represented by this repository.

## What CPA does

CPA maintains a durable project state outside the chat transcript. Source files and user-provided information can be compiled into canonical cognition containing entities, relationships, events, concepts, knowledge, temporal/state information, and provenance. Later conversations retrieve bounded relevant cognition instead of requiring the model to reinterpret the full source on every turn.

For project modifications, normal chat can inspect authoritative `/source`, work through `/workspace`, and create review proposals. Agent tools do not directly overwrite authoritative source files; source edits are staged and require approval through the Review workflow.

## Version 9.5.3 feature set

### Persistent canonical cognition

- Project-scoped cognition persisted independently of chat history.
- Canonical entities, relationships, events, concepts, definitions/knowledge, state, timeline information, and provenance.
- Document and code cognition compilation.
- Additive source recompilation through the `recompile_source` capability.
- Durable compilation overrides are supplied to document compilation so persisted higher-authority state can participate in reconciliation.
- Semantic mutation/reconciliation support for existing cognition.
- Cognition validation, contradiction inspection, impact analysis, state maps, ledgers, world-model snapshots, and session distillation.
- Git-backed pre-compilation/state history support through the project VCS layer.


### Knowledge lifecycle: evidence, hypotheses, decisions, changes, and open questions

Version 9.5.3 turns the project knowledge lifecycle into an active runtime capability rather than leaving these record types as passive storage primitives.

- **Evidence** records concrete observations and their provenance.
- **Hypotheses** represent explanations that are still uncertain and can accumulate supporting or disconfirming evidence.
- **Facts** capture conclusions established strongly enough to be treated as project knowledge.
- **Decisions** preserve a selected approach, its rationale, alternatives, and consequences.
- **Changes** record implemented/approved project changes and connect implementation history back to project reasoning.
- **Open questions** preserve unresolved issues instead of allowing them to disappear when a conversation ends.
- Hypotheses and open questions can be updated/resolved as new information becomes available.
- Substantive investigation and implementation turns expose the lifecycle capabilities so the model can use them during normal work.
- Approved source mutations can create durable change records automatically.

A typical investigation can therefore persist:

```text
evidence
    -> hypothesis
    -> supporting/disconfirming evidence
    -> fact
    -> decision
    -> implementation
    -> review/approval
    -> change record

unresolved branch
    -> open question
    -> later evidence
    -> resolution
```

This makes the reasoning behind a project change retrievable in later sessions instead of preserving only the final code.

### Semantic + structural retrieval

- Local embeddings through FastEmbed.
- `BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5`-based local semantic indexing through the configured FastEmbed stack.
- `sqlite-vec` storage for local vector search.
- One query embedding can be reused across tool/capability, cognition, instruction, prompt-module, and runtime-intent selection.
- Semantic retrieval selects candidate cognition and capabilities.
- Canonical cognition candidates are expanded through structural references rather than treating semantic top-K as the complete answer set.
- Bounded context hydration keeps model context smaller than loading the full cognition store.
- Compatibility fallback remains available when local semantic selection is unavailable.

### Base-chat project execution

- Ordinary chat can recognize imperative project-change requests such as adding UI elements, changing code, or modifying project behavior.
- Project mutation intent receives the project inspection/edit/review capability path without requiring a custom Agent definition.
- The runtime can prime mutation turns with the current source tree.
- Relevant source files can be read before modification.
- Workspace/source-proposal tools are made available for project mutation requests.
- The agent loop tracks successful project mutation/proposal tool calls so a text-only response is not treated as a completed implementation.

### Source / workspace / review boundary

- `/source` is authoritative project material.
- `/workspace` is the AI-editable working area.
- Agent-invoked direct writes are restricted to workspace paths.
- `propose_source_edit` copies and edits an existing source file in workspace and creates a pending review draft.
- `propose_source_file` stages a new source file and creates a pending review draft.
- Review items can be approved, rejected, or edited before approval.
- Approval promotes the reviewed change into authoritative source through the approval layer.

### Project files and format-aware tools

- List and inspect source/workspace trees.
- Read plain-text project files with paging.
- `read_project_text` also extracts readable content from DOCX, PDF, PPTX, and XLSX instead of treating those formats as raw binary text.
- Create, edit, copy, move, rename, and delete workspace files/folders.
- DOCX read/edit tools plus structured DOCX rendering.
- PDF read/write/edit/page-copy tools plus professional structured PDF rendering.
- PPTX tools plus structured 16:9 presentation rendering.
- HTML and Markdown artifact rendering through the shared Document IR.
- Theme-driven artifact transformation with `professional`, `minimal`, `academic`, `report`, and `fantasy_codex` styles.
- Content-preservation validation for document transformation workflows.
- XLSX read/write/edit tools.
- XML tools.
- Regular text/file tools and binary-file support.
- Streamlit file preview for project files, including parsed DOCX preview rather than displaying the underlying ZIP/XML bytes.


### Artifact transformation and professional document rendering

Version 9.5.3 adds a format-independent artifact transformation pipeline for creating clean, structured deliverables from existing project documents without treating formatting as an implicit summarization request.

The pipeline separates document meaning from output formatting:

```text
source artifact
    -> parser
    -> Document IR
    -> operation intent
    -> theme
    -> format-specific renderer
    -> validation
    -> final artifact
```

#### Document Intermediate Representation (Document IR)

CPA can parse source content into a common semantic document model instead of passing a single flattened text blob directly to an output writer. The model preserves document structure such as:

- document metadata and title information;
- sections and heading levels;
- paragraphs;
- ordered and unordered lists;
- tables and table headers;
- callouts;
- images and other supported structured blocks;
- explicit page/section breaks where represented by the source.

The same semantic representation can then be rendered differently for each target format. A heading remains a heading in the IR, while the PDF, PowerPoint, DOCX, HTML, or Markdown renderer decides how that heading should physically appear.

#### Transform, summarize, and generate are separate operations

Artifact requests distinguish three different content operations:

```text
TRANSFORM
existing content -> preserve content -> change presentation/format

SUMMARIZE
existing content -> intentionally reduce content -> new artifact

GENERATE
instructions/sources -> author new artifact
```

For transformation requests such as "clean up this DOCX", "format this professionally", or "export this document as PDF", formatting changes presentation rather than granting permission to silently condense the source. Summarization or rewriting should be explicit when content reduction is intended.

#### Multi-format artifact rendering

The shared artifact pipeline supports format-specific rendering for:

- **PDF** — paginated document rendering with structured typography and layout.
- **PowerPoint (`.pptx`)** — 16:9 presentation rendering with section boundaries, content splitting, continuation slides, and table slides.
- **Word (`.docx`)** — structured Word output with heading, paragraph, list, and table styling.
- **HTML** — semantic HTML with theme-driven presentation.
- **Markdown** — structured Markdown export from the same document model.

This allows one parsed source to drive multiple outputs:

```text
                     -> PDF
                     -> PPTX
source -> Document IR -> DOCX
                     -> HTML
                     -> Markdown
```

#### Theme system

Artifact styling is centralized rather than hard-coded into every transformation path. The included theme set provides:

- `professional`
- `minimal`
- `academic`
- `report`
- `fantasy_codex`

Themes control presentation concerns such as typography, heading hierarchy, spacing, page geometry, table treatment, headers/footers, and other renderer-supported style choices while leaving the semantic source content unchanged.

#### PDF layout improvements

PDF generation is no longer limited to line-by-line canvas text placement for document transformations. The structured PDF renderer supports document-aware pagination and layout, including:

- wrapped paragraphs;
- heading hierarchy;
- automatic page flow;
- tables and table splitting;
- repeated table headers where supported;
- lists and callouts;
- page breaks;
- headers, footers, and page numbers;
- heading/content cohesion such as keeping a heading with following content where possible.

The existing PDF file tools remain available for direct PDF operations, while structured document transformation uses the higher-level rendering path.

#### PowerPoint layout improvements

PowerPoint rendering treats a presentation as a presentation rather than as paginated prose. The renderer can use document structure to:

- create section-oriented slides from heading boundaries;
- use 16:9 slide geometry;
- control text density;
- split oversized content across continuation slides;
- place tables on dedicated slides when appropriate;
- preserve source information across multiple slides for transformation requests instead of silently discarding overflow.

#### Artifact validation

Generated transformations can be checked against the parsed source before delivery. The validation layer is designed to detect content-loss and structural problems such as:

- missing source content;
- missing headings;
- missing structural material;
- suspiciously incomplete transformations;
- renderer-level issues exposed by supported validation checks.

This makes artifact completion a render-and-validate workflow rather than treating successful file creation alone as proof of a correct transformation.

#### High-level document transformation tool

The artifact subsystem exposes a high-level document transformation path so the agent can request an output format and theme without manually constructing low-level PDF or slide coordinates.

Conceptually:

```python
transform_document(
    source_path="WorldBible.docx",
    output_path="WorldBible.pdf",
    theme="fantasy_codex",
    preserve_content=True,
)
```

The parser, Document IR, renderer, and validator remain implementation details behind the tool boundary.


### Git/VCS project history

- Project file history.
- Historical revision retrieval.
- Revision diffs.
- Restore historical content as a new revision rather than rewriting history.
- Explicit project checkpoints.
- API endpoints for history, revision content, diff, restore, and checkpoint operations.

### Persistent instructions

- Project-scoped instruction storage.
- System-scoped instructions remain unconditional runtime invariants.
- Other instructions can be semantically selected for the current request.
- Instructions are manageable through the UI/API layer.

### Agents and workflows

- Persistent project-scoped Agent definitions.
- Agent name, description, objective, instructions, data sources, output targets, allowed/denied tools, workflow steps, trigger metadata, mutation-approval requirement, and enabled state.
- Manual agent execution context shared with the main runtime.
- Deterministic workflow prefix execution support.
- Workflow inspection exposes deterministic vs inference-step boundaries.
- Agent CRUD API endpoints.

### Chat/session persistence

- Persistent project chat sessions.
- Session memory storage and retrieval.
- Tool-call execution traces surfaced by the chat UI.
- Project/session identity carried through the runtime execution context.

### LLM providers

Enabled provider adapters in this snapshot:

- OpenAI
- Azure OpenAI
- Google Gemini
- OpenRouter

Anthropic configuration is recognized, but the provider adapter is explicitly disabled in this installation.

Provider configuration can be stored per project in `.system/config.yaml`; environment variables are also supported. Provider request throttling is project/provider scoped.

### Streamlit interface

The main UI includes:

- Chat
- Files
- Cognition
- Review, including pending-review count
- Instructions
- History
- Settings
- File preview dialog
- Cognition compilation controls and status
- Tool execution trace display
- Project/session navigation

### FastAPI interface

The API exposes operations for:

- Health
- Projects
- Agent queries
- Draft/review approval and rejection
- Instructions
- Git log and project VCS operations
- Ingestion
- Cognition inspection/validation/impact
- Sessions and session memory
- Project file editing/history/revision/diff/restore/move/copy
- VCS checkpoints
- Agent CRUD

## Requirements

- Windows, macOS, or Linux
- Python 3.12.x
- `uv`
- An API key for at least one enabled LLM provider

The project is intentionally constrained to Python 3.12. `pyproject.toml` declares direct dependencies and `uv.lock` records the resolved environment. Commit both when dependency metadata changes.

## Fresh-machine setup

Clone the repository and enter it:

```powershell
git clone https://github.com/Vyshak-NSC/cognitive-agent.git
cd cognitive-agent
```

Create the environment and install the locked dependencies:

```powershell
uv sync
```

`uv sync` creates `.venv` automatically.

Do not manually maintain the environment with ad-hoc `pip install` commands. Add required runtime packages to `pyproject.toml` and regenerate the lock file.

## Environment configuration

Copy the example environment file:

```powershell
Copy-Item .env.example .env
```

Supported provider environment variables include:

- `GEMINI_API_KEY`
- `OPENAI_API_KEY`
- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`
- `AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY`
- `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`

Anthropic credentials can be configured, but the Anthropic runtime adapter is disabled in this snapshot.

Other useful settings include:

- `CHAT_MODEL`
- `OPENAI_MODEL`
- `OPENROUTER_MODEL`
- `JWT_SECRET`
- `PROJECTS_ROOT`
- `MAX_AGENT_STEPS`

Do not commit `.env` or real API keys.

The default local project root is:

```text
./projects
```

